        "payload": "tree b726804160f6bac7618274770460e3d6159b6685\nparent 0c187f399c430219cbb6ce93ecddfd45df29a735\nauthor July-X <over618@163.com> 1792566288 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792566288 +0800\n\nfix(minimax-bot): fold the background job queue behind one line, separate the reply bubbles (#51)\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): fold a long queue of background jobs behind one line\n\nThe task strip had no way to shrink a busy queue: the rows kept the\nofficial shape, but thirty jobs filled the whole strip and the last\nones were only reachable by scrolling inside it.\n\nThe rows stay the ones the official build draws, in its order. What is\nnew is a layer on top: the first five rows stay in the open, the rest\nfold into one line that says how many jobs are left, and a click brings\nthem back. The button sits below the rows' own scroll area, so it\nstays reachable however long the queue is, and the strip keeps the\nheight budget the official one had.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep three job rows in the open in the task strip\n\nThree matches the number the folded reply bubbles keep, and it leaves\nthe transcript more room when a queue is long.\n\n* chore(minimax-bot): bump the version to 0.7.6\n\nThe task strip fold is a build of its own on top of the merged 0.7.5.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): drop the folded working messages, separate the bubbles\n\nThe fold took a long reply's earlier bubbles behind a summary line. The\nqueue of background jobs was the thing that needed folding, so the\nbubbles go back to the flat layout the official build draws: one bubble\nper recorded part, in the order it records them.\n\nWhat was missing there was a gap between them. One reply with many\nparts drew its bubbles flush against each other, so they read as one\nblock of text; a small gap, the one the task rows use, tells them\napart.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep the working jobs in the open, hold the fold button\n\nThe fold of the job queue had three rough edges.\n\nThe button was removed and rebuilt on every render, so the keyboard\nfocus fell back to the body as soon as the queue changed or the clock\nticked. The same node is kept now; only its text and its state follow\nthe queue.\n\nWhich rows stayed in the open was decided by the order the jobs\narrived in, so a job that was already working could be the row that\nfolded away, behind a queue of jobs that had not started yet. The rows\nthat stay in the open are chosen by status now, the working one first,\nwhile the rows themselves keep the order the official build gives them.\n\nAn open queue stayed open after it shrank to a few jobs. It ends with\nthe queue now, and the next busy chat starts folded again.\n\nThe gap between the bubbles of one reply belongs to the bubbles alone.\nThe rule names them instead of the whole column, so the action row,\nthe notes and the event lines keep the spacing the official build gave\nthem. The height budget of the strip and the row the fold button takes\nfrom it are two variables now.\n\n* docs(minimax-bot): describe the folded job queue\n\nThe README described the working messages of a reply but not the strip\nabove the composer, so the fold of a long queue was left undocumented.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 4Dim <m4java@163.com>",
