  {
    "sha": "0c187f399c430219cbb6ce93ecddfd45df29a735",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOUiPFatoAKDBjMTg3ZjM5OWM0MzAyMTljYmI2Y2U5M2VjZGRmZDQ1ZGYyOWE3MzU",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "July-X",
        "email": "over618@163.com",
        "date": "2026-10-21T03:47:16Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "GitHub",
        "email": "noreply@github.com",
        "date": "2026-10-21T03:47:16Z"
      },
      "message": "fix(minimax-bot): fold the earlier bubbles of a long reply, bottom-left reply actions (#48)\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): move reply actions to the bottom-left of the message\n\nThe reaction, copy, quote and open-session buttons used to sit inside the last\nbubble, pinned to its top-right corner. A long reply therefore pushed them out\nof reach while the reader was still at the end of the text, which made quoting a\nlong answer awkward.\n\n- Render the action row as a sibling below the bubble, aligned to its left\n  edge, so it always stays under the last line of the reply.\n- Keep the reaction indicator inside the bubble at its bottom-left, which puts\n  it directly above the action row, and paint it with the app's own tag colours\n  instead of a fixed brand-colour pill so it follows the light and dark themes.\n- Give the transcript enough bottom padding that scrolling to the end never\n  clips the action row of the last reply.\n\nBumps the package to 0.7.5 and aligns the MCP serverInfo version with the\nmanifest.\n\nAssisted-by: mavis reason:minimax-bot-reply-actions\n\n* refactor(minimax-bot): make the background task strip readable at a glance\n\nThe strip put the owner and the state in the same sentence as the task, then\ntruncated the task to one line, so a busy queue read as a wall of near\nidentical rows.\n\n- Lead each row with the task (up to two lines) and put the owner, the state\n  and the elapsed time on a second line.\n- Tell the states apart by their dot: quiet while queued, amber while it\n  waits, accent while it runs.\n- Keep the stop action reachable, but out of the way until the row is looked at.\n- Fold a queue of more than three tasks into one line that can be expanded.\n\nAssisted-by: mavis reason:minimax-bot-task-strip\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): render a reply as one bubble instead of one per recorded part\n\nThe Host opens a text part for every assistant turn between two tool calls, so a reply that ran a dozen tools painted a bubble per part. A real group message in the CodeMap chat has 118 text parts, and the bubbles filled the screen so the conversation above was out of reach.\n\nJoin the text parts of a message into one bubble, the way the server joins them when it stores the message text. The reaction badge follows that single bubble.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep the task strip empty instead of printing null\n\nreplaceChildren renders a null argument as the text \"null\", so every chat without a background job showed that word above the composer. The fold button is appended on its own now, and only when there is something to fold.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): fold the earlier bubbles of a long reply behind a scrollable summary\n\nThe Host records a text part for every assistant turn between two tool calls, so a reply that\nworked for a while arrives with dozens of parts, and one bubble per part filled the screen: a\nreal message in our own Bot history carried 479 parts and 118 text segments.\n\nThe earlier bubbles keep their own shape, but they move into a folded area under the reply: one\nline says how many of them there are, and opening it shows them in a fixed height area with its\nown scrollbar, so the conversation above stays in reach. The answer itself is never folded, and a\nreply with a few parts is unchanged.\n\nThis replaces the earlier attempt to join the parts into a single bubble: that changed how a\nreply reads compared to the rest of the app, and the fold keeps the original bubbles.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep the newest three bubbles of a long reply in the open\n\nThe fold took every bubble but the answer out of sight, so a collapsed\nreply showed nothing of the work that produced it. The newest three\nbubbles now stay where the flat layout put them, and only the earlier\nones fold behind the summary.\n\nA bubble of such a reply also tells on hover what it said, the way the\ntask rows and the attachments do. A short reply keeps the official\nmarkup untouched.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): put the background task strip back to the official rows\n\nThe strip was the one part of this branch that changed the official look\ninstead of adding a layer on top of it: two lines per job, different\nwording, a borderless stop button and a folded queue. The rows render\nthe way the official build does again, one line each, with the stop\nbutton at the end of the row.\n\nMeasured against the official build on the same data: a row is 42px high\nin both, the strip is 88px high in both, and the stop button sits 9px\nfrom the right edge in both.\n\nThe fold of the long reply and the reply actions stay as they are.\n\n* docs(minimax-bot): describe the themed reaction indicator\n\nThe README already says where the reply actions sit; the indicator above\nthat row now wears the app's own tag colours, so the text says so too.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 4Dim <m4java@163.com>",
      "tree": {
        "sha": "1744f40dfd6f7f28b7f625cc27f27728cffe05c2",
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/MiniMax-AI/MiniMax-Code-MiniApps/git/trees/1744f40dfd6f7f28b7f625cc27f27728cffe05c2"
      },
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/MiniMax-AI/MiniMax-Code-MiniApps/git/commits/0c187f399c430219cbb6ce93ecddfd45df29a735",
      "comment_count": 0,
      "verification": {
        "verified": true,
        "reason": "valid",
        "signature": "-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJq2DXECRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAzs4QAKTDKzYheq6k2Z1/9D0AN34u\nQkyF9+7FFnF+zCxJZQXmlSaIdG47B8npg1j/wtE0k3EAFmX0RC8iz+uyzVJ+GXzs\nqLUyIaStrmNCZikO3EsY9giiNJqzJcTvcFyufsMzpXxZask46ET+/XBnZEruW9Fp\nM9YnSXIYOfrawAkMHkYxUC3Z9JQFePizwS3Wo9QG1YBLhOtNRxtdlkU4HbDRE5/r\nthurPHpLYWf8TnF0YsSo20lZ6OYxGHc2Nme/MxNoMzdU9RkmbueHnEg0B184Auf/\nl9LBK5rrhsgjaOMq9ix1xzMNX5I+vOQOm0irNrbQI4O5OZwjhOfMZFpdsQiLPVbr\n64YlEc+Vy0z5+nWG/QY0SiYlZePGaS/br4TXwcLUUZ7Y4FZnvjRxXVb5X3xEo7pD\ncXOCBk71fhPlMNcIOvxnNTLrwA4hL5n+eGDA4QZRQk5rIoIdACgdlsZehyNZClRt\nrXJBfXQwCML9qL5x9gvUyAUYahexfCXVQ/8i3NG/zei/hucR9v0TTbYJLnD+K9Y2\nUuWgPWllNTG0hV3ucZGDhV750BEysAU50csCUouiQ9wwgVmuEtIszMupnIDCgcSj\ngaLisCrj221MBvWKNPo4mnrQ7V6LGlzKjxYzi4IgjwCHyXAl7ZAienZDgCHOUp2I\npXCSbJYwWC70hBiU8VnE\n=M2V+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n",
        "payload": "tree 1744f40dfd6f7f28b7f625cc27f27728cffe05c2\nparent 98040300eb89d18db27057dfa0f3db38c8ff72e8\nauthor July-X <over618@163.com> 1792554436 +0800\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1792554436 +0800\n\nfix(minimax-bot): fold the earlier bubbles of a long reply, bottom-left reply actions (#48)\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): move reply actions to the bottom-left of the message\n\nThe reaction, copy, quote and open-session buttons used to sit inside the last\nbubble, pinned to its top-right corner. A long reply therefore pushed them out\nof reach while the reader was still at the end of the text, which made quoting a\nlong answer awkward.\n\n- Render the action row as a sibling below the bubble, aligned to its left\n  edge, so it always stays under the last line of the reply.\n- Keep the reaction indicator inside the bubble at its bottom-left, which puts\n  it directly above the action row, and paint it with the app's own tag colours\n  instead of a fixed brand-colour pill so it follows the light and dark themes.\n- Give the transcript enough bottom padding that scrolling to the end never\n  clips the action row of the last reply.\n\nBumps the package to 0.7.5 and aligns the MCP serverInfo version with the\nmanifest.\n\nAssisted-by: mavis reason:minimax-bot-reply-actions\n\n* refactor(minimax-bot): make the background task strip readable at a glance\n\nThe strip put the owner and the state in the same sentence as the task, then\ntruncated the task to one line, so a busy queue read as a wall of near\nidentical rows.\n\n- Lead each row with the task (up to two lines) and put the owner, the state\n  and the elapsed time on a second line.\n- Tell the states apart by their dot: quiet while queued, amber while it\n  waits, accent while it runs.\n- Keep the stop action reachable, but out of the way until the row is looked at.\n- Fold a queue of more than three tasks into one line that can be expanded.\n\nAssisted-by: mavis reason:minimax-bot-task-strip\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): render a reply as one bubble instead of one per recorded part\n\nThe Host opens a text part for every assistant turn between two tool calls, so a reply that ran a dozen tools painted a bubble per part. A real group message in the CodeMap chat has 118 text parts, and the bubbles filled the screen so the conversation above was out of reach.\n\nJoin the text parts of a message into one bubble, the way the server joins them when it stores the message text. The reaction badge follows that single bubble.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep the task strip empty instead of printing null\n\nreplaceChildren renders a null argument as the text \"null\", so every chat without a background job showed that word above the composer. The fold button is appended on its own now, and only when there is something to fold.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): fold the earlier bubbles of a long reply behind a scrollable summary\n\nThe Host records a text part for every assistant turn between two tool calls, so a reply that\nworked for a while arrives with dozens of parts, and one bubble per part filled the screen: a\nreal message in our own Bot history carried 479 parts and 118 text segments.\n\nThe earlier bubbles keep their own shape, but they move into a folded area under the reply: one\nline says how many of them there are, and opening it shows them in a fixed height area with its\nown scrollbar, so the conversation above stays in reach. The answer itself is never folded, and a\nreply with a few parts is unchanged.\n\nThis replaces the earlier attempt to join the parts into a single bubble: that changed how a\nreply reads compared to the rest of the app, and the fold keeps the original bubbles.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): keep the newest three bubbles of a long reply in the open\n\nThe fold took every bubble but the answer out of sight, so a collapsed\nreply showed nothing of the work that produced it. The newest three\nbubbles now stay where the flat layout put them, and only the earlier\nones fold behind the summary.\n\nA bubble of such a reply also tells on hover what it said, the way the\ntask rows and the attachments do. A short reply keeps the official\nmarkup untouched.\n\n* fix(minimax-bot): put the background task strip back to the official rows\n\nThe strip was the one part of this branch that changed the official look\ninstead of adding a layer on top of it: two lines per job, different\nwording, a borderless stop button and a folded queue. The rows render\nthe way the official build does again, one line each, with the stop\nbutton at the end of the row.\n\nMeasured against the official build on the same data: a row is 42px high\nin both, the strip is 88px high in both, and the stop button sits 9px\nfrom the right edge in both.\n\nThe fold of the long reply and the reply actions stay as they are.\n\n* docs(minimax-bot): describe the themed reaction indicator\n\nThe README already says where the reply actions sit; the indicator above\nthat row now wears the app's own tag colours, so the text says so too.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: 4Dim <m4java@163.com>",
        "verified_at": "2026-10-21T03:47:17Z"
      }
    },
